7. Brief Introduction to Modern Hardware:

Goal:
To show how computer hardware developed and why some tasks use specialized processors.

7.1 Development of Computer Hardware:
Early electronic computers were large and used many separate electronic components. Over time, transistors and integrated circuits made computers smaller, faster, and more reliable. The development of microprocessors allowed many CPU functions to fit on a single chip.
Today, computers can contain multiple CPU cores, large amounts of RAM, and fast storage. We will only briefly introduce this history because the main focus of this part is how hardware works.

7.2 GPUs and AI Accelerators:
A CPU is designed to handle many different kinds of tasks and instructions. A GPU (Graphics Processing Unit) is designed to perform many similar calculations in parallel. This is useful for graphics and some AI tasks.
An AI accelerator is specialized hardware designed to make certain AI calculations faster or more efficient. For example, some machine-learning tasks involve repeating similar mathematical operations on large amounts of data. GPUs and AI accelerators can be useful for this kind of work.
We do not need to study the internal design of these processors yet. Students only need to understand why computers sometimes use different processors for different tasks.

Activity 13: Which Hardware Would You Choose?
Imagine that you need to process a large number of similar image calculations at the same time. Which hardware would be useful: a CPU or a GPU? Explain why we still need the CPU.

Suggested answer: A GPU can be useful because it can perform many similar operations in parallel. The CPU is still important for running the program and coordinating general tasks. Different tasks benefit from different hardware.
